# Part I — Foundation

## F0. `CustomerId` and the aggregate

**Depends on:** nothing.
**Files:** `src/Common/src/ValueObject/CustomerIdentity.php`, `src/Domain/src/Customer/`.

`CustomerId extends UuidValueObject implements AggregateRootId` in
`Domain\Customer\ValueObject`, and `CustomerIdentity` in `Common` (§0.2). The aggregate
mirrors the layout of
`src/Domain/src/PaymentIntent/`: `Command/`, `Event/`, `Exception/`, `ValueObject/`, plus
`CustomerAggregate.php` and `CustomerAggregateRepositoryInterface.php` (`retrieve` / `persist`).

State:

```
CustomerAggregate
  CustomerId
  CustomerIdentity  — name, email, phone; all #[Pii]
  ?BillingAddress   — the customer's own, distinct from a payment's
```

The payment methods it holds are F1's, added there rather than here so that this task can be
finished and left green on its own.

Events: `CustomerRegistered`, `CustomerIdentityChanged`, `CustomerAddressChanged`,
`CustomerForgotten`.

**No gateway references on the aggregate**, and the reason is not only layering. `GatewayId`
lives in the `Gateway` package, which `Domain` does not depend on (§0.2), so the aggregate
cannot name the type at all — and a `string`-keyed map of provider identifiers would be the
same infrastructure smuggled in untyped. Which customer a provider knows under which id is a
fact about that provider, and F2 gives it a home of its own:
`GatewayCustomerRepository` and the `gateway_customers` table. The aggregate holds identity;
the map holds identifiers.

That leaves the aggregate deliberately thin: an id, who this is, and where they are. Thin is
the point — §0.3 is easier to hold to when there is nothing on the aggregate to hang a
provider concern from.

**Invariants covered by tests:**

- An email is not identity. Two customers may share one; changing it changes nothing about
  which customer this is. This is the defect in `Nuvei/src/CreateCustomerRequest.php:39` and
  the test says so by name.
- A customer with no email is registrable. Today both Stripe and Nuvei make a missing email
  decide whether the customer exists at all; that must stop being expressible.
- `CustomerForgotten` leaves the aggregate replayable: identity reads as stubs and the id
  still resolves. See §0.4 and F7.

**Done when:** unit tests green under `--testsuite=Domain` and `--testsuite=Common`. No
gateway code, no provider names.

## F1. The customer owns its payment methods

**Depends on:** F0.
**Files:** `src/Domain/src/Customer/`.

The aggregate holds the payment methods registered to it — the value objects themselves. §0.3 already names this as one of its
two jobs, and a collection is what that job means.

```
CustomerAggregate
  …
  array<string, PaymentMethod>  — keyed by PaymentMethodId
```

**A payment method and not an "instrument".** `PaymentInstrument` covers cards, tokens, cash
and hosted pages, and all but one of those are transient — a `Token` expires, a `Cash` tender
and a `HostedPayment` are a single payment's shape. Only a payment method is permanent, so only
one can be attached to somebody. The wider word promised room for kinds that can never be held.

**The value objects, not ids or flags.** That is what owning them means, and it is what lets
F8's address deduplication happen at all: a card's billing address can be the customer's only if
the customer is holding the card.

**Payment methods only — not tokens.** A `Token` carries an `ExpiresAt`
(`Common/src/ValueObject/Token.php:21`): it is a transient handle to a card, good for one use
and then gone. A customer's collection of them would fill with dead entries and would imply a
reusability a token does not have. The interface already says these are different kinds —
`tokenize()` and `createPaymentMethod()` are separate operations
(`Gateway/src/Contract/PaymentGatewayInterface.php:60,62`) — and only the second produces
something worth owning.

Worth noting because today it is not so: `EloquentCustomerRepository::visitToken()` returns
the `TokenId` (`:97-100`), so an expiring handle can resolve a customer link. F2 removes that
by keying the map on the customer instead of on an instrument.

**Not a `CustomerId` on `PaymentMethod`.** That was the first draft and it is the weaker
direction, for three reasons:

- **A pointer is not ownership.** With the id on the value object, no aggregate can refuse
  anything. With the collection, the refusals below become expressible at all.
- **`PaymentMethod` is a `Common` value object embedded in payment events.** Adding a field
  rewrites the payload of every `PaymentIntentAuthorized` and needs a read-fallback for ever.
  A collection on a new aggregate touches no existing event.
- **Nothing needs the back-pointer.** The gateway call gets the customer explicitly (F3);
  reporting joins in a projection; erasure iterates from the customer, which is the direction
  that has the list.

**Plain value objects, and explicitly not the `Refund` pattern.** `Refund` is a child
aggregate because events arrive *about a particular refund* — `RefundProcessed`,
`RefundFailed`, `RefundFeeRecorded`, `RefundImported` — and the parent has to route them, so
it boots a child per id (`Domain/src/PaymentIntent/PaymentIntentAggregate.php:748-754`),
registers it with `registerAggregate()`, and the child filters the shared stream by its own id
(`Domain/src/PaymentIntent/Refund/Refund.php:69-76`).

A stored payment method has no such stream. The customer is the one acting on it — it is
registered and detached by the customer's own two events, and nothing else ever changes it. So
the child-aggregate machinery routes nothing: no `AggregateAppliesKnownEvents`, no
`registerAggregate`, no per-child snapshot protocol. The parent's apply methods add and mark
entries in an array, which is all the state there is.

Worth stating because the first draft reached for the nearest precedent instead of asking what
the precedent is for. Child aggregates exist to route events; a value-object collection exists
to hold state the parent maintains itself. This is the second kind.

**No timestamps.** Every message already carries
`EventSauce\EventSourcing\Header::TIME_OF_RECORDING`, so when a card was added is in the stream
already and a `registeredAt` in the payload would be a second copy free to disagree with it.
(That differs from `SubscriptionCancelled::$effectiveAt`, which is on its event because the
moment a cancellation *takes effect* is not the moment it was recorded.)

**Detaching removes the entry**, and nothing is remembered about it. A provider will not take a
released reference back, but that is a fact about the *reference* and belongs to
`GatewayCustomerRepository`; keeping it here would be infrastructure in the aggregate again. A
genuinely new registration of the same card arrives with a new id anyway.

Events: `PaymentMethodRegistered` (carrying the payment method), `PaymentMethodDetached`
(carrying its id).

**Invariants covered by tests:**

- Registering the same payment method twice is refused, not silently deduplicated. Today the same
  card registered twice is two `gateway_references` rows and nothing says they are one card.
- Detaching a payment method this customer does not hold is refused. New refusal +
  `ErrorCode` case.
- `CustomerForgotten` does not detach anything. See F7.

**Done when:** the collection survives a snapshot round-trip — an array of arrays in
`createSnapshotState()`, with no child protocol — every refusal above has a test, and the
suite is green. `Common\ValueObject\PaymentMethod` is not modified by this task.

## F2. Give the customer its own repositories

**Depends on:** F1.
**Files:** `src/Gateway/src/Contract/GatewayCustomerRepository.php` (new),
`src/Gateway/src/Contract/CustomerIdentitySource.php` (new),
`src/Gateway/src/Contract/CustomerRepository.php` (deleted — see F2d),
`src/Laravel/src/Repository/`, two migrations.

`CustomerRepository` is the reason the map was mistaken for a customer model in the first
place (§0). It is named for the *thing* while every sibling is named for what it stores, and
it is keyed by an instrument while every sibling is keyed by the thing it belongs to:

| contract | keyed by | returns |
|---|---|---|
| `GatewayInstrumentRepository` | instrument | that gateway's reference |
| `GatewayTransactionRepository` | payment intent, refund | that gateway's reference |
| `VirtualCardReferenceRepository` | virtual card id | that gateway's reference |
| `CustomerRepository` | **an instrument** | a *customer* reference |

So the customer gets what each of the others already has, and the odd one out goes.

### F2a. The aggregate's repository

`Domain\Customer\CustomerAggregateRepositoryInterface` — `retrieve(CustomerId)` /
`persist(CustomerAggregate)`, exactly the shape of the three that exist
(`PaymentIntent`, `Checkout`, `Subscription`). Declared in F0; this is where it gets its
Eloquent implementation and its own event stream.

This is the customer's actual repository. Nothing about gateways touches it.

### F2b. The gateway reference map

`Gateway\Contract\GatewayCustomerRepository`:

```php
public function find(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $customerId): ?string;
public function saveReference(GatewayId $gatewayId, string $customerId, string $reference): void;
```

Named and shaped like `VirtualCardReferenceRepository`, because it is the same kind of thing.

**Its own table, standing alone.** `gateway_customers(gateway_id, customer_id,
customer_reference)`, UNIQUE on `(gateway_id, customer_id)`. That deletes the pivot's reason
to exist: `gateway_reference_customer` links a customer to a *gateway reference*, which is why
identity is unreachable for a raw card (§0.1) and why a `Token` can resolve one (F1). Full
extraction means the customer's references stop being a satellite of instrument references —
no pivot, no join through `gateway_references`, no shared row.

The UNIQUE is where the "one reference per gateway per customer" rule belongs. It is a
constraint on the map rather than an invariant of the customer: re-pointing a gateway at a
different reference orphans whatever the old one owned, and the table can refuse that without
the domain learning that providers exist.

### F2c. The identity read side

Provider packages need the customer's name, email and address to build a provider-side
customer (F4, F5) and cannot load the aggregate (§0.2). So `Gateway` declares:

```php
interface CustomerIdentitySource
{
    public function find(string $customerId): ?CustomerIdentity;
}
```

Implemented by the host, against the aggregate or against a projection — the host's choice,
and neither is this package's business. The same shape as `TransactionIdResolver`, for the
same reason: `Gateway` states what it needs and stays free of domain types beyond `Common`.

### F2d. Retiring `CustomerRepository`

**Run this after F4 and F5, not before.** Stripe and Nuvei still read the old contract until
those tasks switch them, so removing the interface method first would need a second capability
interface carrying the old repository, existing only to be deleted two tasks later. The
retirement is naturally last.

It is **deleted, not deprecated.** The plan said otherwise and was corrected during the work:
keep it and `findByInstrument()` stays reachable as a silent fallback whenever a caller does not
name a customer — and that lookup is precisely what registered provider-side customers under
whatever address rode along with a payment. A fallback to it makes the whole change optional,
taken by default by every call site that forgot. So the answer for a customer nobody named is
now *no provider customer*, which surfaces as a refused registration rather than an invented one.

Deleted with it: `EloquentCustomerRepository`, `setCustomerRepository()` on
`Gateway\Contract\Gateway`, the repository parameter on both gateway factories, its container
binding, `InstrumentParameters`' accessors, and both pivot relations on the models
(`GatewayCustomer::references()`, `GatewayReference::customers()`).

**The pivot table outlives its reader.** `gateway_reference_customer` is left standing, empty of
readers. It is the only surviving record of which cards a provider knew under one customer, which
makes it the evidence A1 would start from if it ever decides two of our customers are one person.
Kept as history, not as a dependency — nothing in this package reads it, and nothing should start
to. Dropping it is A2's call, once the host has taken what it needs.

**One consequence that reaches every provider package.** `setCustomerRepository()` is on
`Gateway\Contract\Gateway` itself (`src/Gateway/src/Contract/Gateway.php:16`), so all five
gateways implement it — Stripe and Nuvei use it, ConnexPay injects it unread, Paynet and
Revolut only satisfy the signature. Retiring the contract changes that interface method, and
the three that never used it should lose it rather than be given a new one to ignore. A
gateway that has no customer concept should not be made to declare that it has.

**Done when:** the aggregate persists and replays through F2a, the new map answers by
`CustomerId`, both migrations are listed in `hasMigrations` (a stub in the directory alone
does nothing — `Laravel/src/GatewayServiceProvider.php:96`), no code path resolves a provider
customer from an instrument, and the suite is green.

## F3. The customer reaches the gateway call

**Depends on:** F2.
**Files:** `src/Gateway/src/Contract/PaymentGatewayInterface.php`,
`src/Gateway/src/PaymentGatewayRouter.php`, `src/Domain/src/PaymentIntent/Port/Request/CreateRequest.php`.

`authorize()`, `charge()` and the rebilling variants take `?string $customerId = null` — a
string, for the reason §0.2 gives, and alongside `clientUniqueId` which is one too.

**On the signature, not on the credential.** A customer is a fact about this payment, which
is what distinguishes it from `authenticationUrl` and `returnUrl` — those are one address per
deployment and belong in configuration
(`Stripe/src/StripeGateway.php::setAuthenticationUrl`). It sits with `clientUniqueId`,
`billingAddress`, `description` and `initiation`, which are per-payment facts too.

Whether it also rides `CreateRequest` and therefore the domain: **yes, but as an id only.**
The aggregate does not read it and records nothing from it; it is carried so the port can
pass it on, the way `billingAddress` is. Adding a method to `CreatePaymentIntentCommand` is
breaking for every host implementation — say so in the task, once.

**Done when:** the router forwards it, a gateway that ignores it still passes its tests, and
the suite is green.

## F4. Stripe: the lookup replaces the search

**Depends on:** F3.
**Files:** `src/Stripe/src/StripeGateway.php`, `CreateCustomerRequest.php`.

`resolveCustomerReference()` becomes: look up this customer's reference for this gateway; if
absent, create a Stripe Customer from the identity read through `CustomerIdentitySource`
and link it. The `BillingAddress`-derived branch goes.

`adoptCustomerFromStripe()` **stays**, demoted. It is the repair path for a crash between the
