# Part II — Application

Not implemented in this repository. Listed because Part I's events have to be sufficient for
it; if a task here cannot be built from what Part I emits, that is a Part I bug.

- **A0. Wiring.** `CustomerIdentitySource` (against the aggregate or a projection — the host's
  call) and passing a `CustomerId` at every gateway call site. The
  `CustomerAggregateRepositoryInterface` implementation ships with the package.
- **A1. Identity resolution.** Which person a request belongs to — lookup, dedupe, merge.
  Explicitly the application's (§0.3); foundation takes the id it is given. **Nothing downstream
  works without it**, and it has nothing to resolve *from* yet: the application has no person
  entity, so after A2 there is one customer per card and no rule that says a returning payer is
  one of them. Whatever that rule turns out to be — email, an account the application grows, a
  merchant-supplied id — it is the thing that makes every other customer task worth having, and
  it is not something a migration can decide on the way past.
- **A2. Backfill.** Give every existing payment method a customer that holds it, i.e. emit F1's
  `PaymentMethodRegistered` for each. The application's, because that is where the payment
  methods are — `payment_methods`, `billing_addresses`, the `source` morph. Tokens stay out of
  scope for the same reason they are out of F1: the expired ones are most of them.

  This was built in the foundation once and taken back out, and the findings are worth more than
  the code was:

  - **One customer per payment method, and no grouping.** `gateway_reference_customer` links a
    payment method to a provider-side customer, so it is tempting to treat a card linked at two
    gateways as proof those two provider-side customers are one person, transitively. That is a
    defensible reading of the table and still the wrong thing for a migration to do: it decides
    who someone is from data never collected to answer that, permanently, and a wrong merge
    cannot be undone — while two customers that are one person can be merged later.
  - **Leave `gateway_customers.customer_id` null.** With one customer per card, a row covering
    three cards has three candidates and no answer. A null there costs nothing that is not
    already lost: an unnamed customer resolves to no provider customer today too. The column
    fills as customers are named on new activity.
  - **Make the customer reachable, or the backfill is write-only.** Ids anchored on row ids are
    ids nothing will ever look up — at payment time a caller holds a payment method, not a
    `gateway_customers.id`. Either derive the customer id from the payment method's (UUIDv5 over
    a fixed namespace, so a re-run lands on the same customer and the aggregate's refusal of a
    duplicate registration reads as "already moved"), or record the mapping as A2b. Whichever:
    the derivation stops being the answer the moment A1 merges two customers.
  - **A row that cannot be reconstructed must be named, not skipped.** A shredded card has
    nothing truthful to register, and registering a placeholder would put a card in a customer's
    stream that never existed — but a silent omission reads as "there were none".
  - `CustomerIdentity::fromBillingAddress()` is the reading it needs, and it ships with `Common`.
- **A2b. Payment method → customer projection.** F1 deliberately has no back-pointer, so "whose
  card is this" is a read model rather than a field. Nothing in the foundation asks it any more —
  F2d deleted the lookup that did — so this is for the application's own reads, and it is what
  has to hold the answer once A1 merges customers and A2's derivation stops being one.
- **A3. Nuvei re-keying.** Every existing Nuvei customer is keyed by email (F5). Either
  re-register each customer under its UUID and re-tokenize the cards, or keep the email token
  for pre-existing customers and use UUIDs only for new ones. The second is less work and
  leaves two conventions in the data; the choice belongs to whoever owns the migration
  window, not to this plan.
- **A4. Erasure surface.** The endpoint or job that triggers `CustomerForgotten`, and the
  audit of who asked.

